BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Contemplation by Design / Stanford Prevention Research Center] CB
 D 2026: Lectio Divina (Becoming Chanting)\, Dental Floss\, and AI: How Con
 templation Happens and Why It Matters When Your Intelligence Is Not Artifi
 cial  with Carmen Acevedo Butcher\, PhD
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T073000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T081500
DTSTAMP:20261019T190315Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53047985797143-53048005291018@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Lectio divina\, or “sacred reading\,” and dyslexia are wor
 d siblings\, as lectio and dyslexia both come from the ancient root *leg- 
 “to collect or gather.” As someone with dyslexia and as a survivor of 
 trauma\, Carmen has found the ancient monastic practice of steeping in sac
 red texts an always accessible\, lifelong balm for collecting and connecti
 ng with her inner wholeness and resting in her gentlest Self. Carmen often
  finds this practice leads to chanting as she walks through the marsh. Fol
 lowing\n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://med.stanford.edu/contemplation/s
 ummit.html\nLast synced: 2026-10-19 12:03 PDT
URL:https://med.stanford.edu/contemplation/summit.html
END:VEVENT
